================================================================================
PART 9: TRANSITIONING TO DYNAMIC DATA
================================================================================

PREPARING FOR API INTEGRATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Separating static data can make later API integration conceptually cleaner:

• Similar Data Shape
  - API responses can be mapped to the structure expected by components

• Replaceable Source
  - Static imports can later be replaced with fetched data

• Limited Component Changes
  - Presentational components may continue receiving the same props

• Scalability
  - Data no longer needs to be maintained directly in source files

• Flexibility
  - Application data source can evolve


================================================================================
EXAMPLE: TRANSITIONING TO API DATA
================================================================================

BEFORE - STATIC DATA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

import { useState } from 'react'
import { booksData } from './data'


export default function App() {

  const [books, setBooks] = useState(booksData)

  // ...
}


AFTER - API DATA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

import { useEffect, useState } from 'react'


export default function App() {

  const [books, setBooks] = useState([])


  useEffect(() => {

    // Fetch data from API

    fetch('https://api.example.com/books')
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(data => setBooks(data))
      .catch(error =>
        console.error('Error:', error)
      )

  }, [])


  // ...
}


STATIC VS API FLOW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

STATIC DATA:

books.js
   ↓
booksData
   ↓
useState(booksData)
   ↓
books
   ↓
map()
   ↓
Book Component


API DATA:

API
 ↓
fetch()
 ↓
response.json()
 ↓
setBooks(data)
 ↓
books State
 ↓
map()
 ↓
Book Component


BENEFITS OF THIS APPROACH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Limited UI Changes
  - The Book component can continue consuming book props

• Easier Testing
  - Static data can still be useful during development/testing

• Gradual Migration
  - Static data can be replaced when the backend is available

• Fallback Development Data
  - Local sample datasets can remain useful for development

• Separation
  - UI components are less dependent on where data originates


